  "doc.title": "How Pixels Reach a Screen · a long essay you can play with",
  "doc.desc": "A 4K screen refreshing 120 times a second has to deliver nearly a billion pixels a second, each lit the right colour at the right moment. How do they get from a block of GPU memory, through one cable, to light in front of your eyes, and why does the picture tear or stutter? Eleven hands-on models: how many bytes a frame takes, the pixel clock from real timing tables, a tearing and vsync timeline, variable refresh with low framerate compensation, the colour pipeline and its lookup tables, whether a cable fits a given mode, what 4:2:0 throws away, how long a row of pixels has to charge, motion blur when the eye tracks, the HDR PQ curve, and the latency from mouse click to light.",
