  "hero.title": "How Pixels Reach a Screen",
  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.sub": "A 4K screen that refreshes 120 times a second needs nearly a billion pixels a second.<br>How do they get from a block of memory on the graphics card, down one cable, to the light in front of you?",
  "hero.lead": "3840 × 2160 × 120 comes to {pxPerS} pixels a second: {pxPerSyi} × 10⁸, just short of a billion. Each one has to light up in the right colour at the right moment. A little late and you get a torn line across the picture; one frame late and you get a stutter. The story below follows the pixel. It starts as a few bytes in video memory, then the <strong>display engine</strong> reads them out one row at a time on a strict timetable, stacks other layers on top, converts them to the screen's colours, and packs them into a serial stream of bits sent down a DisplayPort or HDMI cable. At the other end, the <strong>timing controller</strong> in the screen hands them out to thousands of row and column lines, and the transistor in each pixel charges up within a few microseconds while the liquid crystal turns or the organic material lights up. Two questions run through the middle: <strong>why a picture tears or stutters</strong>, and <strong>how long it takes from a mouse click to the screen lighting up</strong>. <strong>Each model is a small version of something real</strong>: the pixel clock comes from the CTA-861 timing table in the Linux kernel, the timelines step through Vulkan's present modes frame by frame, and the PQ curve is computed from SMPTE ST 2084.",
  "hero.note": "This page was written by an AI. Its rules come from primary sources (the Linux kernel's drm_edid.c, drm_dp.h and KMS documentation, the CVT formula in edid-decode, VESA's published material on DisplayPort, DSC and eDP, the HDMI Forum's HDMI 2.2 figures (as reported by the trade press; the specification itself was not opened), ITU-R BT.2100 and BT.709, and Microsoft's documentation on the DXGI flip model), and each one says where it came from and when. <strong>Values marked “tuned parameter” were chosen so the model can calculate; they were not measured</strong>, and the text says how the conclusion changes when one is doubled. This page does not rank any brand and gives no buying advice.",
  "ch1.title": "One frame is one block of memory",
  "toc.ch1": "One frame is one block of memory",
  "ch1.law": "A frame on the screen is, on the graphics card, a block of bytes in video memory: a few bytes per pixel, laid out row after row. Every refresh, this whole block is read from start to finish, whether or not the picture has changed.",
  "ch1.p1": "Think of the card stunt at a stadium opening ceremony: thousands of people each hold a coloured board, and from the stands a picture appears. Each person has a sheet saying which colour to hold at each second. The graphics card's <strong>framebuffer</strong> is that sheet: a block of video memory that says, row by row, what colour each pixel should be.",
  "dg.overview.cap": "The route a pixel takes (schematic, not to scale). The left half is on the graphics card, the right half is in the screen, and only one cable joins them. The frame the GPU has drawn sits in video memory. The display engine reads it out on the timetable, stacks the layers and converts the colours, then hands it to the encoder to become a serial signal. The timing controller (TCON) in the screen takes it in and passes it to the row and column drivers.",
  "dg.overview.aria": "The route of a pixel: the framebuffer in video memory, the display engine, the encoder and one cable, then the timing controller and row and column drivers in the panel, and finally the pixels",
  "dg.overview.vram": "VRAM",
  "dg.overview.fb": "Framebuffer (2–3)",
