  "ch1.p2": "The number of bytes one pixel takes is its <strong>pixel format</strong>. The most common, RGBA8, gives 8 bits each to red, green, blue and transparency, so 4 bytes per pixel. RGB10A2 squeezes transparency into 2 bits and gives each colour 10 bits, still 4 bytes. For HDR and linear-light compositing, FP16 is used: a 16-bit floating-point number per channel, 8 bytes per pixel.",
  "dg.fmt.cap": "How many bits one pixel takes in memory (bit widths drawn to scale). RGBA8 and RGB10A2 are both 32 bits per pixel; the latter takes 6 of alpha's 8 bits and gives the three colours 2 more each. FP16 is 64 bits, for holding HDR and linear light.",
  "dg.fmt.aria": "Bits per pixel in memory for three pixel formats: RGBA8 has 8 bits per channel, RGB10A2 has three 10-bit channels plus 2 bits, FP16 has a 16-bit half-precision float per channel",
  "dg.fmt.rgba8": "RGBA8 · 32 bits",
  "dg.fmt.rgb10": "RGB10A2 · 32 bits",
  "dg.fmt.fp16": "FP16 · 64 bits",
