  "ch1.p3": "The stored numbers are usually not the light intensity itself but the result of a curve, typically the sRGB curve. The reason is the dark end: the eye is more sensitive to differences in dark areas. Take the darkest 1% of light: {darkSrgb} of the 256 sRGB codes fall in that range, but storing linear intensity directly leaves only {darkLin} codes for it, and the dark areas break into visible bands. So the numbers in the framebuffer are <em>encoded</em>; before blending or lighting you must convert them back to linear light (the display engine in chapter 5 does this).",
  "ch1.p4": "The GPU draws these bytes: vertices, rasterisation, pixel shading, and finally writing into the framebuffer. For that stage, see <a href='../how-a-gpu-computes/#ch9'>“How a GPU Computes”, chapter 9</a> and <a href='../../graphics/computer-graphics/'>“Computer Graphics · From Coordinates to Pixels”</a>. This essay starts from the moment the writing is done.",
  "ch1.model.title": "Model 1 · How big a frame is, and how many bytes a second it reads",
  "ch1.model.how": "Choose the resolution, pixel format, refresh rate and number of buffers. See how much video memory one frame takes, and how many bytes the screen reads from video memory each second.",
  "ch1.ctl.res": "Resolution",
  "ch1.res.fhd": "1080p",
  "ch1.res.qhd": "1440p",
  "ch1.res.uhd": "4K",
