  "ch1.read.px": "Pixels a second (visible only)",
  "ch1.read.bw": "Read from video memory per second",
  "ch1.say.ok": "At every refresh, the display engine reads this {frame} from start to finish, {hz} times a second, which is <em>{bw}</em>. Even if the picture does not move at all, the reading never stops.",
  "ch1.say.fp16": "FP16 takes 8 bytes per pixel, so the same picture doubles the bytes read: <em>{bw}</em> per second. That is why an HDR frame is usually converted to 10 bits in the last step before it is sent out.",
  "ch1.say.huge": "This is already <em>{bw}</em> read continuously, just to send the same picture out again and again. The bandwidth of video memory can cope, but the power cost is real. Chapter 12 explains how a laptop screen can make it stop.",
  "ch1.p5": "4K, RGBA8, 120 Hz, three buffers: one frame is {frameMiB} MiB, the three buffers total {fb3MiB} MiB, and {readGBps} GB/s is read each second. In the same format at 60 Hz it is {read60} GB/s; with FP16 it is {fp16GBps} GB/s. Against the total bandwidth of video memory, which runs to several hundred GB/s, this is a small share (for the memory itself, see <a href='../how-memory-talks/#ch9'>“How Memory Talks to the CPU”, chapter 9</a>). But it has two special features: <strong>it cannot be late</strong>, because the screen will not wait; and <strong>it never stops</strong>, because it reads even when the picture has not changed. The display engine has its own buffer in front that reads a little early and keeps the data, precisely to guard against video memory being briefly busy.",
  "ch1.limit": "This model counts only the framebuffer itself. Real drivers also use compressed formats (each graphics vendor has its own lossless compression, which reads fewer bytes), tiled storage, and separate layers for the cursor and video, which chapter 5 covers.",
  "ch2.title": "Read row by row: the pixel clock",
  "toc.ch2": "Read row by row: the pixel clock",
  "ch2.law": "The display engine reads the framebuffer one pixel at a time, on a timetable: after each row of visible pixels there is a stretch of blank time, and after each frame's visible rows there is more. Visible plus blank, multiplied by the refresh rate, gives the pixel clock.",
  "ch2.p1": "An old typewriter, after finishing a line, had to push the carriage back to the left and advance the paper before typing the next line. That time spent pushing back typed nothing. Display timings keep the same blank time, called <strong>blanking</strong>. Originally the beam of a cathode-ray tube had to sweep back from right to left and from bottom to top, and it had to be switched off during that time.",
  "ch2.p2": "Today's panels have no electron beam, but the blank time is still there because it has other uses: HDMI carries audio and information packets about the picture inside it, and the circuits in the screen use it to align each row and frame. Variable refresh, in chapter 4, works by stretching it. In one row there are, in order, the visible pixels, the <strong>front porch</strong>, the <strong>sync pulse</strong> and the <strong>back porch</strong>; the rows in a frame are arranged the same way.",
  "dg.raster.cap": "The timing of one frame, drawn in both directions (the numbers come from CTA-861 mode 97, which the models on this page use; the proportions are schematic). You read the way you read text: finish one row from left to right, return to the start of the row during blanking, then read the next row. After the last row, return to the top left during vertical blanking.",
  "dg.raster.aria": "Timing of one frame: the visible area is at the top left; to its right, in order, are the row front porch, sync and back porch; below, in order, are the frame front porch, sync and back porch",
  "dg.raster.act": "Visible area 3840 × 2160",
  "dg.raster.hfp": "Front porch {hfp97}",
  "dg.raster.hs": "Sync {hs97}",
  "dg.raster.hbp": "Back porch {hbp97}",
  "dg.raster.vfp": "Front porch {vfp97}",
  "dg.raster.vs": "Sync {vs97}",
  "dg.raster.vbp": "Back porch {vbp97}",
  "dg.raster.htot": "{h97} clocks a row",
  "dg.raster.vtot": "{v97} rows a frame",
  "dg.raster.scan": "Reading direction",
  "dg.raster.hb": "Row blanking",
  "dg.raster.vb": "Frame blanking",
  "ch2.p3": "The timings used by TVs and HDMI devices are registered in the CTA-861 standard, with one number for each resolution and refresh rate; the Linux kernel's drm_edid.c copies the whole table. Mode 97 is 4K at 60 Hz: a row has 3840 visible pixels, a front porch of {hfp97}, sync of {hs97}, a back porch of {hbp97}, for a total of {h97}; a frame has 2160 visible rows, a front porch of {vfp97}, sync of {vs97}, a back porch of {vbp97}, for a total of {v97} rows. {h97} × {v97} × 60 gives a pixel clock of exactly {pclk97} MHz.",
  "ch2.model.title": "Model 2 · Pixel clock from a real timing table",
  "ch2.model.how": "Choose a timing table, then drag the front and back porches. Grey in the picture is blanking, gold is the visible area. Read the pixel clock, the time for one row, and how long the vertical blanking lasts. Once you change a value, it is no longer a standard timing.",
  "ch2.model.aria": "The total pixel area of one frame, drawn to scale, with the visible part in gold and the blanking in grey",
  "ch2.cv.act": "Visible",
  "ch2.cv.blank": "Blanking",
  "ch2.cv.sync": "Sync pulse",
  "ch2.ctl.preset": "Timing table",
  "ch2.p.cta16": "1080p60 · CTA 16",
  "ch2.p.cta97": "4K60 · CTA 97",
